"local": [
{
"title": "Find her in Singapore",
"text": "Our own bridge builder is the golden orb web spider, Nephila pilipes. NParks records it as common across Singapore, along roadsides and in parks. Her web is huge and its silk shines gold in the sun. Every orb web starts the way Mrs. Spider's bridge does, with one thread carried across a gap by the breeze."
},
{
"title": "The boy with the kite",
"text": "The man in the story was the engineer Charles Ellet Jr. In 1848 he needed a first line across the Niagara gorge, about 240 metres wide, between the United States and Canada. A 16-year-old named Homan Walsh flew his kite across. His kite string pulled over a cord, then a rope, then a wire cable, and the first bridge was built on it."
},
{
"title": "On your next walk",
"text": "Look for a single thread stretched between two plants or across a path, with no web on it yet. That is a bridge line. Look for it early in the morning, when dew makes the silk easy to see."
}
],
